# Apple Design Principles

Source: https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/design-principles
(reintroduced June 8, 2026 — supersedes "Clarity / Deference / Depth" and the legacy six themes)

Use these to weigh competing priorities and break ties. They are criteria, not rigid rules.

- **Purpose** — Make something meaningful. At every stage, ask what the product is for and whether the design serves that purpose. Investigate existing solutions instead of recreating them.
- **Agency** — Stay out of the way. Allow free exploration instead of locking people into flows or modes, and **always make mistakes recoverable**. Recovering from the unexpected must not cost people their time or work.
- **Responsibility** — Be transparent about what the product does and why. Collect only what the product needs to function.
- **Familiarity** — Build on concepts people already know. Once an appearance or behavior is established, apply it consistently everywhere. Give clear feedback: show when controls are available and when content changes.
- **Flexibility** — Treat accessibility as a priority from the start, never as a final patch. Preserve context by keeping content and controls in consistent, predictable positions and adapting to window and screen size. Support multiple input methods.
- **Simplicity** — Simplicity is **not** minimalism. Keep the important things close and let the others fall away. Be concise. Always establish an explicit hierarchy.
- **Craft** — Be deliberate with every decision. Prototype early and discard what doesn't work. Shipping is not the finish line.
- **Delight** — Decide which emotion the experience should evoke. Never mistake delight for decoration: it must not get in the way of the core task.

## Non-negotiables

Destructive actions must be reversible (undo) or explicitly confirmed, and must never share
visual styling with routine actions.

A dialog that warns "this change may cause inconsistencies, consider resetting" is an Agency
violation: it hands a software problem to the user. Fix the underlying behavior instead.

```
// BAD — pushes the problem onto the user
onModeToggled() {
    showWarning("Existing edges are not updated. Consider clearing everything.");
}

// GOOD — the software absorbs the complexity
onModeToggled() {
    rebuildDerivedStateFromSourceOfTruth();
}
```
